&lt;p&gt;Key observations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Linear memory growth&lt;/strong&gt;: RSS consumption grew predictably from 420MB to 638MB under doubled load, with no memory leaks detected over the 14-minute test window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;p99 latency degradation&lt;/strong&gt;: At 100 concurrent users, p99 latency breached our 3000ms threshold by 5%, primarily due to database connection pool saturation (default 10 connections).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Error rate spike&lt;/strong&gt;: Failed requests jumped from 1.2% to 4.1% during the spike phase, traced to webhook timeout defaults (30s) colliding with AI gateway retries.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Production Threshold
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For workflows processing synchronous webhooks, n8n maintains acceptable performance up to approximately 50-60 concurrent executions per 4-core node before requiring horizontal scaling. The architecture becomes economically viable when:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need visual workflow building with versioning (vs. hardcoding orchestration logic)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Execution volume exceeds 100k workflows/day (amortizing self-hosting overhead)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Integrations span 10+ SaaS tools (avoiding custom OAuth/API clients)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below 20k executions/month, serverless alternatives like AWS Step Functions offer better cost efficiency. Above 500k/day, you'll need PostgreSQL read replicas and Redis-backed queue management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Configuration Hardening
&lt;/h2&gt;

